-- chem/tailblock.cub — a lower bound from a finite span and a bounded tail (docs/chemical-identity.md, "The lower
-- bound is one square over a solved base", (b)). Two finite facts, each an identity between polynomials, checked on a
-- grid finer than its degree.
-- (1) The block inequality. A vector is x = p + q, p in a finite span P, q in its complement Q; the form of H − λ is
--   p·F·p + 2 q·b + q·T·q,   F = P(H−λ)P, b = Q(H−λ)p, T = Q(H−λ)Q.
-- If q·T·q ≥ c·(q·q) with c > 0, then c times the form is at least c·(p·F·p) + c·(c·(q·q) + 2 q·b), and
--   c·(c·(q·q) + 2 q·b) + b·b = (c q + b)·(c q + b)                                  (`tb-square`, per component)
-- so c·(form) ≥ c·(p·F·p) − b·b: H − λ ≥ 0 on everything when c·F − BᵀB ≥ 0 on P, B the map p ↦ b, BᵀB =
-- P H Q H P = P H² P − (P H P)². For one function on each side it reads c(F p² + 2Bpq + c q²) = (cF − B²)p² +
-- (cq + Bp)² (`tb-block`). The vector statement is the sum of `tb-square` over components; `tb-vec` is that sum
-- computed at dimension three, an instance.
-- (2) The tail constant. In H₀'s own states, a vector with weight w₀ on the lowest state φ₀ (level E₀) and weight w
-- on states at level E has ⟨H₀⟩ = E₀w₀ + E·w and norm W = w₀ + w, and
--   E₀w₀ + E·w − (E₁ − (E₁−E₀)d)·W = (E − E₁)·w + (E₁ − E₀)·(d·W − w₀)             (`tb-tail`)
-- so if every other level is at least E₁ > E₀ and w₀ ≤ d·W, then ⟨H₀⟩ ≥ (E₁ − (E₁−E₀)d)·W. For q in Q, w₀ =
-- ⟨q, φ₀⟩² = ⟨q, Qφ₀⟩² ≤ ⟨q, q⟩·‖Qφ₀‖², which is w₀ ≤ d·W with d = ‖Qφ₀‖²: Cauchy–Schwarz, an identity in the plane
-- of the two vectors, (x·x)(y·y) − (x·y)² = (x₁y₂ − x₂y₁)² (`tb-cs`). With k states φ_i at levels E_i below E_{k+1}
-- and d_i = ‖Qφ_i‖², the same with a sum: the constant is E_{k+1} − Σ(E_{k+1} − E_i)d_i (`tb-tailk`, the identity
-- for lists, checked at k = 2). Two electrons without repulsion at charge Z have levels −(Z²/2)(1/n₁² + 1/n₂²):
-- E₀ = −Z², E₁ = −5Z²/8 (`tb-e0`, `tb-e1`), and the constant is −5Z²/8 − (3Z²/8)d, −5/2 for helium at d = 0
-- (`tb-he`). Taken here, not proved: that 1/r₁₂ ≥ 0 as an operator, so QHQ ≥ QH₀Q; H₀'s decomposition into its own
-- states with those levels (the continuum included, all at or above E₁ once φ₀ is removed); and the passage from
-- the component identities to operators on the function class.
import "barta.cub"

import "linalg.cub"

